THE HEADLINE LOAD RATIO: SEVEN DIFFERENT PERCENTAGES, AND WHICH ONE TO QUOTE
============================================================================
All are "% of the DC-10's recorded 2.70 g peak-to-peak" (TM-102186 Fig. 6,
-1.0 to +1.7 g). They differ because the RUNS differ. They are NOT inconsistent
reports of one run. A presentation that quotes the wrong one is wrong.

 %      p-p(g)   configuration                                          source
------ -------- ------------------------------------------------------ -----------------
 68.1%  1.8390  HEADLINE. Mehta 5-vortex field, `boeing747`, full       PROJECT.md:327
                model, all gradient terms. scripts/cat_validation.py.   (and :2568)
 67.1%  1.8124  AtiSim TRANSLATIONAL ONLY - gradient terms deliberately PROJECT.md:716
                DISABLED for like-for-like parity with JSBSim, on
                `boeing747_jsbsim` at JSBSim's start state.
 76.0%  2.0517  AtiSim + omega_gust only                                PROJECT.md:717
 74.1%  2.0011  AtiSim + BOTH gradient terms (its real configuration)   PROJECT.md:718
 74.6%  2.0152  JSBSim - INDEPENDENT ENGINE, same field/aircraft/state  PROJECT.md:715
 72.7%  1.9631  Most favourable corner of the V0/r0 uncertainty band    PROJECT.md:557
 70.1%  1.8918  Session-26 wing-loading sweep baseline, FIRST CORE ONLY PROJECT.md:1000

VERIFIED ARITHMETIC (recomputed from the ledger's own n_z values):
  headline: 1.441 - (-0.398) = 1.839 ; 1.839/2.70 = 68.11%   <-- matches "68%"

THE DOCUMENTATION DEFECT (real, but minor and NOT a fabricated number):
  PROJECT.md:101 (section 1, the validation-claim paragraph) says
    "The headline load comparison reaches 67% of a recorded peak-to-peak"
  67% is the LIKE-FOR-LIKE TRANSLATIONAL figure (67.1%, line 716), not the
  headline. The headline is 68.1%. Section 1 attaches the right number to the
  wrong run. Five other occurrences in the document say 68%.

WHAT TO QUOTE IN THE PRESENTATION:
  "About two thirds (68%) of the recorded peak-to-peak load" for the headline run.
  If the JSBSim cross-check is mentioned, quote 74.6% (JSBSim) vs 67.1% (AtiSim
  like-for-like) AS A PAIR and say the comparison is deliberately handicapped -
  AtiSim's own full configuration reaches 74.1%, i.e. within 0.5 points of JSBSim.

THE RESULT THAT MATTERS MOST, AND IT IS NOT THE PERCENTAGE:
  NEITHER engine reaches the record. An independent, mature flight-dynamics code
  given the same wind field, the same aeroplane and the same starting state also
  falls ~25% short. That excludes AtiSim's SOLVER as the explanation for the
  shortfall, on the headline field, by measurement rather than by inference.
